## Identities and domains

For integer n >= 2, direct distribution telescopes
(a-b) sum[j=0..n-1](a^(n-1-j)b^j) = a^n-b^n.
This works for every real a,b, including zeros and equal inputs; it does not
rely on the incomplete special-case argument in source block 6.
For n=3 this is the difference-of-cubes formula; substituting -b yields
the sum-of-cubes formula. Block 9's worked example is not a selected concept.
All algebraic variables here are real. Rational expressions retain the ORIGINAL
domain after cancellation. Positive quadratic factors introduce no extra real
exclusions: u^2 +/- uv + v^2 vanish only when both variables are zero.

## 309

Task: put (x-10)(x^2+10x+100) into standard polynomial form by BOTH methods.
Distribution: x^3+10x^2+100x-10x^2-100x-1000 = x^3-1000.
Identity: a=x, b=10 gives x^3-10^3 = x^3-1000.
No domain exclusions. The important distinction is cancellation of mixed terms,
and recognition that the last constant is cubed, not squared.

## 310

Task: standard-form products; each substitution is independently evaluated.
a: a=x,b=2 in difference of cubes -> x^3-8.
b: a=p,b=5 -> p^3-125.
c: a=y,b=4 in sum of cubes -> y^3+64.
d: a=2a,b=1 in sum of cubes -> 8a^3+1.
e: a=10x,b=3y in difference of cubes -> 1000x^3-27y^3.
f: a=4a,b=5b in sum of cubes -> 64a^3+125b^3.
These are polynomial identities on all real inputs.
Meaningful units: scalar/one-variable applications a-d; scaled two-variable
applications e-f, where whole monomials (including coefficients) must be cubed.

## 311

Task: factor each concrete binomial by the appropriate cube identity.
a: (x-y)(x^2+xy+y^2).
b: (c-2)(c^2+2c+4).
c: (x+3)(x^2-3x+9).
d: (2x-3)(4x^2+6x+9).
e: (5a+1)(25a^2-5a+1).
f: (n-4m)(n^2+4nm+16m^2).
g: (y/2-10)(y^2/4+5y+100).
h: (x+1/3)(x^2-x/3+1/9).
i: (c-0.1)(c^2+0.1c+0.01).
j: (0.2b+5)(0.04b^2-b+25).
k: (0.4-ny)(0.16+0.4ny+n^2y^2).
l: (4x+y/3)(16x^2-4xy/3+y^2/9).
These equalities have no real-domain exclusions.
All decimal quantities above are exact terminating decimals, not approximations.
Meaningful units: a-f integer cube bases; g,h,l rational cube bases;
i,j,k decimal cube bases. These distinguish coefficient extraction and sign,
not twelve repetitions of the same click.

## 312

Task: prove all four exact divisibility claims without numerical approximation.
a: 327+173=500, so 327^3+173^3
=500(327^2-327*173+173^2)=500*80287; the quotient is an integer.
b: 731-611=120, so 731^3-611^3
=120(731^2+731*611+611^2)=120*1354323.
c: 4759-2779=1980, so 4759^3-2779^3
=1980(4759^2+4759*2779+2779^2)=1980*43596183.
d: 386+214=600, NOT 1200. The quadratic factor is
386^2-386*214+214^2=112188=2*56094.
Thus 386^3+214^3=600*112188=1200*56094.
Alternatively put 386=2*193 and 214=2*107: the cubic sum equals
8*300*(193^2-193*107+107^2)=1200*2*28047.
The extra factor 2 is essential in d.
Response unit: judgment of complete integer-factor proof certificates for all
four claims. Loss: independently inventing and writing those proofs.

## 313

Task: exact efficient evaluation of both original radical expressions.
a: 59-41=18. Difference-of-cubes division gives
59^2+59*41+41^2; adding another 59*41 gives (59+41)^2.
The principal square root is 100.
b: 67+52=119. Sum-of-cubes division gives
67^2-67*52+52^2; subtracting another 67*52 gives (67-52)^2.
The principal square root is |67-52|=15.
Unit: the ordered pair of exact results for the two complementary identities.

## 314

Task: reduce four rational expressions, preserving exclusions.
a: (y^3-1000)/(y-10)=y^2+10y+100, y != 10.
b: (8x^3+27)/(6x+9)
=(2x+3)(4x^2-6x+9)/[3(2x+3)]
=(4x^2-6x+9)/3, x != -3/2.
c: (a^2+6a+36)/(a^3-216)=1/(a-6), a != 6.
The canceled quadratic (a+3)^2+27 is strictly positive.
d: (18b^2+24b+32)/(27b^3-64)
=2(9b^2+12b+16)/[(3b-4)(9b^2+12b+16)]
=2/(3b-4), b != 4/3.
The canceled quadratic is (3b+2)^2+12 > 0.
Unit: a complete reduced-expression/domain table, not separate blank filling.

## 315

Task: combine each original expression into one fraction.
a: let S=p^2+pq+q^2. Common denominator (p-q)S gives
(S-3pq)/[(p-q)S]=(p-q)^2/[(p-q)S]=(p-q)/S.
Original real domain is p != q. S=0 only at p=q=0, already excluded.
The simplified expression would otherwise allow many p=q points; do not
silently restore them.
b: let S=a^2-2a+4=(a-1)^2+3.
Numerator on denominator (a+2)S is
6a^3+12a-3a^2(a+2)=3a^3-6a^2+12a=3aS.
Result 3a/(a+2), a != -2.
c: let S=x^2+x+1=(x+1/2)^2+3/4.
Common numerator:
2x^2+7x+3 -(1-2x)(x-1) -3S
=2x^2+7x+3 -(-2x^2+3x-1) -3x^2-3x-3
=x^2+x+1=S.
Result 1/(x-1), x != 1.
d: let S=y^2+2y+4=(y+1)^2+3.
Common numerator:
(y-2)^2-6y+S=2y^2-8y+8=2(y-2)^2.
Result 2(y-2)/S, y != 2. At y=2 the reduced expression is defined,
but the original is not.
Meaningful units: two-term combinations a,b; three-term combinations c,d
with a sign-distribution and removable-domain trap.

## 316

Task: express each sixth-power expression as a product; irreducible
factorization is not required by the written seed.
a: (x^2+y^2)(x^4-x^2*y^2+y^4), by cubes of x^2,y^2.
b: (a^2-b^2)(a^4+a^2*b^2+b^4), by cubes of a^2,b^2.
c: (c^2-1)(c^4+c^2+1).
d: (n^2+1)(n^4-n^2+1).
e: (y^2-4)(y^4+4y^2+16).
f: (4k^2-n^2)(16k^4+4k^2*n^2+n^4).
All are polynomial identities for all real variables.
For b another legitimate route is (a^3-b^3)(a^3+b^3), and full
factorization is (a-b)(a+b)(a^2+ab+b^2)(a^2-ab+b^2).
For c,e,f difference-of-squares and further cube factorizations are also
legitimate; the choice must not label an equivalent product false merely
because its number of factors differs.
Units: sum-of-cubes products a,d; difference-of-cubes products b,c,e,f.

## 317

Task: binomial times trinomial, retaining the composed cube bases.
a: A=a+6,B=1 -> (a+5)[(a+6)^2+(a+6)+1]
=(a+5)(a^2+13a+43).
b: A=y-2,B=3 -> (y+1)[(y-2)^2-3(y-2)+9]
=(y+1)(y^2-7y+19).
c: A=x+3,B=4 -> (x-1)[(x+3)^2+4(x+3)+16]
=(x-1)(x^2+10x+37).
d: A=x,B=x-y -> (2x-y)[x^2-x(x-y)+(x-y)^2]
=(2x-y)(x^2-xy+y^2).
All variables real; no exclusions. One complete four-row table retains
the common nested-substitution task.

## 318

Task: apply the general telescoping identity, not only its cubic special case.
a: a=x,b=1,n=6 -> x^6-1.
b: a=y,b=2,n=4 -> y^4-16.
c: a=a,b=3,n=5 -> a^5-243.
The exponent is one larger than the leading exponent in the second factor.
All identities are valid on all real inputs, including x=1,y=2,a=3.
Unit: the three concrete standard-form outputs as one coherent result.

## 319

Task: cancel a difference-of-powers factor with its original domain.
a: x^4+x^3+x^2+x+1, x != 1.
b: (y^6+y^5+y^4+y^3+y^2+y+1)/2, y != 1,
because 2y-2=2(y-1). The factor 1/2 is not canceled.
Unit: the ordered pair of quotient/domain results.

## 320

Task: judge BOTH stated correspondence directions using actual figure pixels.
Opened exactly once each: durable source fig-32.png and complete 640px modern
preview. Both show A(3,5), B(1,2), C(6,1), segments AB and BC, dashed vertical
projections and closed endpoints. No source point D or extension exists.
AB: y=(3/2)x+1/2 for x in [1,3].
BC: y=(11-x)/5 for x in [1,6].
a: input is a POINT on the polyline; output is that point's x-coordinate.
Every input point has exactly one output. This is a function even though
different points may share an x-coordinate. Its image is [1,6].
b: input is a real number in [1,6]; output would be a point at that x.
For 1<x<=3 there are two different points, one on each segment.
At x=2 they are (2,7/2) and (2,9/5); at x=3 they are A and (3,8/5).
At x=1 the segments meet in the SAME point B, not two distinct outputs.
For 3<x<=6 only BC supplies a point. Thus b is not a function:
coverage of all inputs is insufficient without uniqueness.
Auxiliary N18/N19 lookup did not locate relevant definition prose. No
exercises or solutions from them were read. PROVISIONAL terminology assumption:
a function may have points as domain elements, requires exactly one output
per input, and need not be injective. The visible stem will state this criterion.
The parent has assigned retained DEFINITION evidence to a separate reviewer;
the author does not claim that the source's terminology has been approved.
Unit: one multiple-selection task on the shared unchanged image.

## 321

Task: draw the full graph and find the image of the CLOSED interval [-1,6].
Complete square: y=(x-1)^2-16, upward-opening parabola; axis x=1;
vertex (1,-16); zeros -3 and 5; y-intercept (0,-15).
The full function has domain R and range [-16,infinity).
For [-1,6], vertex lies inside; endpoint values are f(-1)=-12 and f(6)=9.
It decreases from x=-1 to 1 and increases from 1 to 6.
Continuity gives EXACTLY [-16,9], with both endpoints included.
Unit: a completed graph-feature and interval-image specification.
Loss: no independent construction of the graph is evidenced, and no
candidate graph artwork is authored under this finite assignment.

## 322

Task: prove both expressions rational by exact expansion.
a: squares are 49+20sqrt(6) and 49-20sqrt(6), so their sum is 98.
b: both radicands are positive since 7>2sqrt(10); therefore
sqrt(7+2sqrt(10))*sqrt(7-2sqrt(10))=sqrt(49-40)=3.
The outer square is 14+2*3=20.
Both results are integers, hence rational. Principal roots are used.
Unit: the ordered exact-value pair; independent written proof is not
demonstrated by selecting it.

